                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Sometimes, great indie games are very hard to find until I dig all the way through. That's HeartZ.\n\nAlthough it can be played from single player up to 3 local co-op players, HeartZ's gameplay is strongly emphasis on 3 players. I had to grab two of my siblings to play it together.\n\nWhat I remember from this game is we were yelling and pointing fingers at each other (in a fun way) for making slight mistake at the cost of us replaying whole section again.\n\nMost of the puzzles are challenging and required thinking and discussion on what characters should do and what order. Sometimes precision is needed and requires several trial and error (and hotdogs... I mean deaths). However the game has a minor problem of displaying in 2.5D bwtween our characters and level layout, and may cause unexpected mismoves or deaths. Each characters has its own specialty to progress the level, which none is more useful nor useless than others. \n\nGraphics are decent, keeping characters and map level bright with nice colours pallette.\n\nHeartz has a simple story (and developers seems to have a fun sense of humor). The game doesn't explain through voice acting nor subtitles, but only visuals and sound effects. Honestly I was hoping another game, Unmechanical, shows more visual story as much as HeartZ.\n\nControls are OK. Each characters have few buttons to memorise and it's enough to enjoy without getting frustrated about controls. I only co-HOPE its response time and turning speed would be better.\n\nDisappointed this game has very few ambient music, none of which adds the atmosphere of the game.\n\nOverall, despite several flaws, we had a lot of fun playing this simple yet challenging co-op puzzle platformer game. Strongly recommended playing HeartZ with your friends or family. 7.5/10 (for 3 players).",
